    "description": "Access to public services in Iran depends on eligibility, affordability, physical accessibility and continuity of provision. Health care, education and basic infrastructure illustrate why formal coverage and practical access require separate assessment.",
    "tags": ["Public services", "Health care", "Education", "Accessibility", "Refugees", "Water and sanitation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T04:39:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T04:39:22Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Access to Public Services in Iran: Health, Education and Inclusion",
      "description": "Examine practical access to health care, education and essential infrastructure in Iran, including documentation, disability and emergency-related barriers."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "meaning-of-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From eligibility to usable services",
        "content": "A service can exist without being equally usable by everyone. For readers examining Iran, several questions need separate answers: who qualifies, what costs remain, whether buildings and materials are accessible, and whether provision continues during disruption. Refugee health arrangements, disability-inclusive education and emergency reporting show different parts of this picture. None provides a complete measure of access across the country. [s1][s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "health-coverage",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Primary care and insurance are different",
        "content": "UNHCR, the UN Refugee Agency, describes support for primary health care for refugees and forcibly displaced people regardless of documentation status. Its Iran health information page lists vaccination, maternal and child health, essential medicines and other primary-care services. It separately describes access by government-registered refugees to the Universal Public Health Insurance scheme, which provides subsidized secondary and tertiary care. [s1]\n\nThese arrangements should not be collapsed into a claim that all treatment is free. The same source distinguishes refugees whose insurance premiums receive UNHCR support from those expected to pay their own premiums. An entitlement to primary care, insurance enrollment and the costs associated with more specialized treatment therefore concern different stages of access. The source describes the scheme; it does not independently measure waiting times, medicine availability or household spending. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "education",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Enrollment and meaningful participation",
        "content": "UNHCR’s education guidance describes documentation, an introduction letter, local school capacity and required assessments as elements of school enrollment for foreign students. This is administrative guidance rather than evidence that every eligible child obtains a place. Its wording also needs a historical caveat: an undated live page cannot establish the exact procedure in force on the editorial cutoff. [s2]\n\nFor children with disabilities, access extends beyond admission. UNICEF identifies shortcomings in accessible infrastructure, learning equipment and materials. Earlier research by Human Rights Watch and the Center for Human Rights in Iran documented inaccessible facilities, missing accommodations and inadequate teacher preparation. That report drew on interviews and research undertaken before publication in 2019; it supports a historical account of barriers, not an assumption that every documented practice continues unchanged. [s3][s4]\n\nTogether, these sources suggest a useful distinction: enrollment records describe entry into education, while accessible classrooms, materials and support determine whether participation is workable. [s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "continuity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Infrastructure and continuity",
        "content": "OCHA’s humanitarian update covering March and early April 2026 reported damage to civilian infrastructure and disruption of electricity, water and telecommunications during hostilities and flooding. Its sector reporting describes how water-system damage and power outages could also interrupt health services. This illustrates the dependence of one public service on another. [s5]\n\nUNICEF’s August response account subsequently described mobile health facilities, vaccine cold-chain support, water treatment and hygiene assistance. These activities document a response to disruption; they do not establish complete restoration or equal access across affected provinces. A sound assessment therefore needs both evidence of assistance delivered and evidence about remaining needs. [s6]",
        "source_ids": ["s5", "s6"]
      }
    ],
